# 🧠 First Principle (Don’t skip this)

GDP = **flow of economic activity**, not profit, not hype, not tweets.

So your job is:
👉 Track **where money is spent**
👉 Track **who earns it**
👉 Track **how fast it circulates**

---

# 1️⃣ EXPENDITURE ACCOUNT

(*Where the money is spent*)

This is closest to the classic GDP formula:
**GDP = C + I + G + (X - M)**

But adapted for a movie.

---

## 🎬 A. Investment (I) — Production Side

This is the *birth of the movie economy*

Track:

* Production budget (sets, VFX, salaries)
* Pre-production costs (writing, scouting)
* Equipment rentals
* Studio costs
* Insurance, legal

**Data parameters:**

* Total budget
* Domestic vs international spend split
* % paid to local vendors (important for GDP impact in India)

👉 This is the cleanest GDP contribution. No debate.

---

## 🍿 B. Consumption (C) — Audience Spend

This is where people over-index.

Track:

* Box office ticket sales
* OTT subscriptions (if tied to release spike)
* Merchandising (t-shirts, collectibles)
* Food & beverages in theatres

**Data parameters:**

* Gross box office (domestic vs overseas)
* Avg ticket price × footfall
* Ancillary spend per viewer (F&B multiplier)

👉 Important nuance:
Ticket revenue ≠ full GDP
Only the **value-added portions** count (theatre margins, distributor share)

---

## 🏛️ C. Government (G)

The silent partner that always eats.

Track:

* GST on tickets
* Income tax from cast/crew
* Corporate tax from production houses
* Local permits, shooting fees

**Data parameters:**

* Effective tax rate
* Total tax collected linked to film lifecycle

---

## 🌍 D. Net Exports (X - M)

This is where things get interesting.

Track:

* Overseas box office earnings
* Streaming rights sold internationally
* Foreign investment in production
* Imports (foreign VFX studios, equipment)

**Data parameters:**

* Foreign revenue inflow (X)
* Foreign spend leakage (M)

👉 A big global hit = export engine
👉 A VFX-heavy Hollywood-dependent film = import leakage

---

# 2️⃣ INCOME ACCOUNT

(*Who actually earns the money*)

Now we flip perspective.

Instead of “who spent”, we ask:
👉 “Who got paid?”

---

## 💼 A. Wages & Salaries

Track:

* Actors, directors, technicians
* Daily wage workers (lightmen, drivers, extras)
* Marketing teams

**Data parameters:**

* Total wage bill
* Distribution across skill levels

👉 This connects directly to your IoV thinking
Skill-based income distribution = real story

---

## 🏢 B. Profits

Track:

* Production house profits
* Distributor margins
* Theatre profits
* OTT platform gains

**Data parameters:**

* EBITDA estimates
* Profit share agreements

---

## 🏦 C. Rent & Interest

Often ignored but real.

Track:

* Equipment leasing
* Studio rentals
* Financing costs (loans taken for production)

---

## 🧾 D. Taxes (again, from income side)

* Income tax from individuals
* Corporate tax

---

👉 Key insight:
A ₹100 crore movie where 80% goes to 2 actors
vs
A ₹100 crore movie spread across 10,000 workers

Both same GDP
Completely different **economic quality**

